+++ b/grocery/capture-policy-lib.ps1
@@ -1309,12 +1309,268 @@ function Test-CaptureLanded {
   return $false
 }
 
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# DID THE CAPTURE COVER THE SLICE THE CURSOR IS ABOUT TO ADVANCE PAST? (2026-09-19)
+#
+# THE DEFECT, replayed out of out\capture-cursor-log.jsonl against the captures of those days:
+#   Sam's Club 2026-08-22  #0  -> #7   122 fresh rows landed, for 7 terms, and NOT ONE of them in #0-#6
+#   Fareway    2026-08-22  #14 -> #21  7 fresh rows landed, for 7 other terms; the file's other 905 rows
+#                                      were CARRIED, dated 2026-07-14 to 2026-08-21
+#   Walmart    2026-08-30  #28 -> #35  525 fresh rows landed, every one for a term outside #28-#34
+# Each of those slices then waited a whole 90-day rotation, and some of their terms have no captured row
+# to this day.
+#
+# WHY THE OLD TEST COULD NOT SEE IT. Test-CaptureLanded asks a question about the FILE - does this store's
+# everyday file for today exist and hold rows - and the answer is yes in two situations that have nothing
+# to do with the slice:
+#   1. THE FILE IS A CARRY FILE. out\regular\<prefix>-regular-<date>.json is rebuilt daily from the
+#      previous day's rows plus today's, so it holds rows whether or not anything was captured today.
+#   2. THE CAPTURE WAS REAL AND WAS OF SOMETHING ELSE. An expiry re-price, a standing ruling's recapture,
+#      a rescue rebuild or a hand capture all land fresh rows for terms that are not the rotation slice.
+# "Rows landed" and "the slice was captured" are two different questions, and only the second is the
+# cursor's business. Test-CaptureLanded still answers the first, and is still the gate in front of this.
+#
+# WHAT COUNTS AS EVIDENCE, strongest first. Every tier is something the capture already records:
+#   asked     the lane passes -AskedTerms: what it actually put to the store this run. The only tier
+#             open to a lane that commits the cursor BEFORE writing its file, which the Baker's lane
+#             does on purpose (a refused write must not also refuse the rotation - see its header).
+#   receipts  the file carries capture_terms, one entry per term with an outcome. success or empty both
+#             mean the term was ASKED and the store answered, and that is the whole point of preferring
+#             receipts: a term the store does not stock is covered by its empty answer, so it can never
+#             freeze the rotation. Baker's, Family Fare and Hy-Vee write these today.
+#   rows      the file's rows carry found_by_term and as_of. A slice term is covered when a row dated
+#             TODAY was found by it. Weaker, in a known direction: a term asked that answered nothing is
+#             indistinguishable from a term nobody asked (.claude\rules\grocery.md - UNCHECKED IS NEVER
+#             NOT-CARRIED), so this tier UNDER-counts coverage and the cursor moves more slowly than it
+#             might. Under-counting costs a repeated request; over-counting costs a quarter of coverage.
+#   none      the capture records neither, so the slice cannot be looked at. That is NOT scored as a
+#             failure - a could-not-look must not settle the question - so the run falls back to the
+#             landing test exactly as it behaved before, and says so in its reason.
+#
+# AND HOW FAR IT MAY MOVE. The cursor advances to ONE PAST THE LAST COVERED TERM of the slice and never
+# further. There is no threshold and no tuning constant in that rule, which is the point of it: whatever
+# IS covered still moves the cursor, so an uncovered term cannot freeze the rotation, and the terms past
+# the last covered one are not stepped over but lead tomorrow's slice. A slice with nothing covered holds
+# the cursor exactly where it is, which is what the old test already promised it would do.
+#
+# STATE WHAT THAT DOES AND DOES NOT PROMISE. A term the capture never reached at all - everything past
+# the last covered one - is never stepped over, and those are the whole slices the three incidents
+# skipped. A term INSIDE the covered span that shows no coverage IS stepped over, and is read as asked
+# and answered nothing: the capture demonstrably reached the terms on both sides of it. On the receipts
+# tier that reading is exact (the receipt says empty, or says not_asked and is then not covered at all);
+# on the rows tier it is an inference, and it is the one the vocabulary cannot settle. Advancing the
+# whole slice on a stop-at-the-first-gap rule would be the safer-sounding choice and is the wrong one:
+# one genuinely not-carried term at the head of a slice would then hold that store's rotation for ever,
+# which is the Hy-Vee deadlock of 2026-08-22 wearing a different coat.
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+# An outcome that means the term was ASKED and the store answered. Deliberately the same vocabulary as
+# $script:BakersAdAskedOutcomes, which reads the same receipts for the ad list; the self-test asserts
+# the two lists are equal so they cannot drift into two meanings of "asked".
+$script:SliceAskedOutcomes = @('success', 'empty')
